{"type": "answer", "run_id": "overload-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "overload", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q3", "request_id": "overload-20261110T183141Z-0012", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:28.470+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 47.179, "patience_s": 4.48, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 2122.1, "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "overload-20261110T183141Z-0012", "question": "How are the positional encodings built, and why sinusoids rather than learned embeddings?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "The Transformer adds a positional encoding vector to each token embedding, where the encoding has the same dimension \\(d_{\\text{model}}\\) and is defined by sine and cosine functions of varying frequencies: for even indices \\(2i\\) it uses \\(\\sin(\\text{pos}/10000^{2i/d_{\\text{model}}})\\) and for odd indices \\(2i+1\\) it uses \\(\\cos(\\text{pos}/10000^{2i/d_{\\text{model}}})\\) so that each dimension corresponds to a sinusoid whose wavelength forms a geometric progression [S1].  \nThese sinusoidal encodings were chosen because a fixed offset \\(k\\) between positions can be expressed as a linear function of the original encodings, making it easy for the model to learn relative positions [S1].  \nThe authors also tried learned positional embeddings and found that performance was nearly the same as with sinusoids [S2].  \nHowever, they preferred the sinusoidal version because it can generalize to sequence lengths longer than those seen during training, whereas learned embeddings are limited to the lengths they were trained on [S1][S2].", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 p.6 §3.5", "pid": "attention:2400:0011", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p06-b004", "attention-p06-b005", "attention-p06-b006", "attention-p06-b007", "attention-p06-b008", "attention-p06-b009"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706